  "query_preview": "(Saturday through Tuesday)...\nTropical Storm Marie continues to strengthen and is expected to\nbecome a hurricane today while continuing on a track toward the\nnorthwest. In the ensemble guidance, there continues to be a \ngradual slow shift toward the south and west with the highest \ntrack density. There continues to be somewhat greater spread in \nthe track guidance once Marie reaches the longitude of Point \nConception. \n\nThe most likely scenario remains that the core of Marie will\nremain well to the southwest of the area. Swell from Hurricane\nMarie is expected to bring an increase in surf to southern\nCalifornia beaches during the weekend with the biggest surf for \nbeaches in Orange and San Diego Counties from Oceanside northward.\n\nEven with the relatively drier solutions, those with the core of \nMarie remaining well to the southwest, increase precipitable water\nvalues to more than one inch over the weekend. At the drier end \nof the guidance, the NBM 10th percentile precipitable water value \nat San Diego increases from around 1 inch for early Saturday \nmorning to 1.7 inches for early Sunday morning with the\ncorresponding 50th percentile values at the same times 1.5 and 2 \ninches.\n\nThe low pressure system near the West Coast could draw some of the\nmid and high level moisture from Marie into southern California\nand the Great Basin bringing a chance of showers and a slight \nchance of thunderstorms. Over time from Saturday into Sunday, \nwhere that might occur would be moving slowly northward with time \nwith the rainfall potential increasing with time as well with the \nincreasing moisture content of the atmosphere. However, there \nremains larger spread in the details. \n\nNights should be warmer for the holiday weekend. High temperatures\nfor some inland areas could be a little cooler depending on the\namount of moisture influx and cloud coverage.",
